        <section class="col-lg-9">
﻿@{
    Layout = "~/Areas/GitHub/Views/Shared/GithubLayout.cshtml";
    ViewData["Title"] = "Inspecting Git History";
}

<div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
    <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">

        <!-- HEADER -->
        <header class="mb-4">
            <h2 class="fw-bold mb-3">Inspecting Git History</h2>

            <p class="mb-2 lead text-muted">
                As your project grows, your Git repository will contain many commits.
                Git provides powerful commands that help you review what changed,
                understand when changes were made, and identify who made them.
            </p>

            <p class="text-muted">
                In this lesson, you will learn how to view your commit history,
                customize the output of <code>git log</code>, and inspect the
                changes introduced by a specific commit.
            </p>
        </header>


        <!-- SECTION 1 -->
        <section class="mb-4">
            <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">
                1. View the Commit History
            </h4>

            <p>
                The <code>git log</code> command is one of the most useful commands
                for exploring the history of a Git repository.
                It displays commits in reverse chronological order, with the
                newest commit appearing first.
            </p>

            <p>
                A standard commit entry includes the commit hash, author,
                date, and commit message.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block mb-3 shadow-sm p-3 bg-dark text-white rounded font-monospace">
                <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git log
            </div>

            <div class="alert alert-light border">
                <strong>Tip:</strong>
                If your repository does not contain any commits yet,
                <code>git log</code> will not display a commit history.
                Create your first commit before using this command.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 2 -->
        <section class="mb-4">
            <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">
                2. Understanding the Git Log Output
            </h4>

            <p>
                When you run <code>git log</code>, Git displays information about
                each commit. Understanding this information makes it easier to
                track changes and troubleshoot your project.
            </p>

            <div class="table-responsive mb-3">
                <table class="table table-bordered align-middle">
                    <thead class="table-light">
                        <tr>
                            <th>Information</th>
                            <th>Description</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Commit Hash</strong></td>
                            <td>
                                A unique identifier for the commit.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Author</strong></td>
                            <td>
                                The person who created the commit.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Date</strong></td>
                            <td>
                                The date and time when the commit was created.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                        <tr>
                            <td><strong>Commit Message</strong></td>
                            <td>
                                A short description explaining what changed.
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 3 -->
        <section class="mb-4">
            <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">
                3. Useful Options for <code>git log</code>
            </h4>

            <p>
                Large projects can contain hundreds or thousands of commits.
                Instead of viewing everything, you can use options to customize
                the output and quickly find the information you need.
            </p>

            <ul>
                <li class="mb-3">
                    <strong>Show changes with <code>-p</code></strong><br />
                    Displays the actual line-by-line changes introduced by each commit.
                    This is useful when reviewing code changes.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-3">
                    <strong>Limit commits with <code>-n</code></strong><br />
                    Displays only the specified number of recent commits.
                    For example, <code>git log -2</code> shows the latest two commits.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-3">
                    <strong>Compact output with <code>--oneline</code></strong><br />
                    Displays each commit on a single line using a shortened commit hash
                    and commit message.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-3">
                    <strong>Display file statistics with <code>--stat</code></strong><br />
                    Shows which files were changed and summarizes the number of
                    lines added and deleted.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-3">
                    <strong>Visualize branches with <code>--graph</code></strong><br />
                    Displays an ASCII graph that helps you understand branches,
                    merges, and the relationship between commits.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </section>

        <!-- TERMINAL SIMULATION -->
        <section class="mb-4">
            <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">
                4. Try It in the Terminal
            </h4>

            <p>
                The following example shows how a repository's history might look
                when using the compact <code>--oneline</code> format.
            </p>

            <div class="card bg-dark text-white font-monospace mb-3 shadow">
                <div class="card-header bg-secondary text-light d-flex justify-content-between align-items-center py-2">
                    <div class="d-flex align-items-center">
                        <span class="bg-danger rounded-circle me-1"
                              style="width:12px;height:12px;display:inline-block;"></span>

                        <span class="bg-warning rounded-circle me-1"
                              style="width:12px;height:12px;display:inline-block;"></span>

                        <span class="bg-success rounded-circle me-3"
                              style="width:12px;height:12px;display:inline-block;"></span>

                        <span>Terminal — Git History</span>
                    </div>

                    <span class="badge bg-dark border border-light text-uppercase">
                        Bash
                    </span>
                </div>

                <div class="card-body">
                    <div class="mb-2">
                        <span class="text-secondary">$</span>
                        <span>git log --oneline</span>
                    </div>

                    <div class="text-light">
                        a84f21c Update student registration API<br />
                        7c91de4 Add payment validation<br />
                        35ab821 Create student module<br />
                        18f5d90 Initial project setup
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="alert alert-info border-0">
                <strong>What does this mean?</strong><br />
                Each line represents one commit. The value on the left is the
                shortened commit hash, followed by the commit message.
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 5 -->
        <section class="mb-4">
            <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">
                5. Git Log Quick Reference
            </h4>

            <div class="table-responsive">
                <table class="table table-hover table-bordered align-middle">
                    <thead class="table-light">
                        <tr>
                            <th scope="col" style="width: 27%;">Command</th>
                            <th scope="col" style="width: 43%;">What It Shows</th>
                            <th scope="col" style="width: 30%;">Best Use</th>
                        </tr>
                    </thead>

                    <tbody>
                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <code>git log</code>
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Complete commit information including hash,
                                author, date, and message.
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                <span class="badge bg-primary">
                                    Detailed History
                                </span>
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <code>git log --oneline</code>
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                A compact, one-line summary of each commit.
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                <span class="badge bg-info text-dark">
                                    Quick Overview
                                </span>
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <code>git log -p</code>
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Shows the actual line-by-line changes introduced
                                by each commit.
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                <span class="badge bg-warning text-dark">
                                    Code Review
                                </span>
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <code>git log --stat</code>
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Shows changed files and the number of lines
                                added or removed.
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                <span class="badge bg-success">
                                    Change Summary
                                </span>
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <code>git log --graph</code>
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Displays a visual text-based representation
                                of branches and merges.
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                <span class="badge bg-primary">
                                    Branch Visualization
                                </span>
                            </td>
                        </tr>

                        <tr>
                            <td>
                                <code>git log -5</code>
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                Displays only the five most recent commits.
                            </td>
                            <td>
                                <span class="badge bg-secondary">
                                    Recent Changes
                                </span>
                            </td>
                        </tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 6 -->
        <section class="mb-4">
            <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">
                6. Inspect a Specific Commit
            </h4>

            <p>
                Sometimes you already know which commit you want to investigate.
                Instead of reviewing the entire history, you can use
                <code>git show</code> with the commit hash.
            </p>

            <div class="code-block mb-3 shadow-sm p-3 bg-dark text-white rounded font-monospace">
                <span class="text-secondary">$</span> git show [commit-hash]
            </div>

            <p>
                This command displays detailed information about the selected
                commit, including the commit message and the changes made to files.
            </p>

            <div class="alert alert-success border-0">
                <strong>Example:</strong>

                <div class="mt-2 font-monospace">
                    git show a84f21c
                </div>

                <div class="mt-2">
                    Here, <code>a84f21c</code> represents the commit you want
                    to inspect.
                </div>
            </div>
        </section>

        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <section class="mb-3">
            <h4 class="fw-semibold mb-3">
                7. Practical Workflow
            </h4>

            <p>
                A simple workflow for inspecting project history is:
            </p>

            <ol>
                <li class="mb-2">
                    Run <code>git log --oneline</code> to get a quick overview.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Identify the commit you want to investigate.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Run <code>git show [commit-hash]</code> to inspect its changes.
                </li>

                <li class="mb-2">
                    Use <code>git log -p</code> when you need to review changes
                    across multiple commits.
                </li>

                <li>
                    Use <code>git log --graph</code> when you need to understand
                    branches and merges.
                </li>
            </ol>
        </section>

        <!-- KEY TAKEAWAY -->
        <section>
            <div class="alert alert-dark border-0 shadow-sm mb-0">
                <h5 class="fw-bold mb-2">
                    <i class="bi bi-lightbulb me-2"></i>Key Takeaway
                </h5>

                <p class="mb-0">
                    Git history is more than a list of commits. By combining
                    <code>git log</code>, its useful options, and
                    <code>git show</code>, you can understand how your project
                    evolved, investigate previous changes, and quickly find
                    the source of a problem.
                </p>
            </div>
        </section>
